    perf(core): drop the seal and the per-key WeakSet from the lazy internals (#6435)

    * perf(core): drop the seal and the per-key WeakSet from the lazy internals

    #6429 restored two behaviours with two mechanisms: a $constructor-level
    seal, so a derived constructor could replace what its base installed on
    the shared prototype, and a WeakSet per key to break recursive reads.
    Both cost bundle size, which lands hardest on zod/mini.

    Override only has to be legal inside a single init chain, and that is
    visible from the internals being installed — no symbol, no per-constructor
    flag, and no hook in $constructor. The recursion break moves to one shared
    accessor descriptor that shadows the key while its getter runs, so no key
    allocates a WeakSet and the memoization rule is unchanged.

    zod-mini-object -71 B raw / -50 B gzipped, zod-object -70 B / -41 B.

    * fix: a throwing compute memoizes nothing

