commit 4d6b5cd3eb36e650fec0586bc085ed7263d43ef4
Author: Colin McDonnell <3084745+colinhacks@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Sun Aug 16 15:14:36 2026 -0700

    fix(json-schema): route unrepresentable default values through `unrepresentable`

    A bigint `.default()` crashed `toJSONSchema` with a raw `JSON.stringify` TypeError before any hook could run. `defaultProcessor` was the one unrepresentable site that never called `handleUnrepresentable`, so neither `override` nor an `unrepresentable` handler could reach it — the only way through was patching `_zod.def.defaultValue`.

    Detect a bigint anywhere in the default value and route it through the same helper every other site uses, so the existing semantics apply: a handler may substitute one, `"any"` drops it, and `"throw"` reports a named error instead of a V8 TypeError.

    closes #6209

diff --git a/packages/docs/content/json-schema.mdx b/packages/docs/content/json-schema.mdx
index ee9f4870..8e67e00f 100644
--- a/packages/docs/content/json-schema.mdx
+++ b/packages/docs/content/json-schema.mdx
@@ -276,6 +276,20 @@ z.toJSONSchema(schema, {

 Two schemas can share a `zodSchema` but not a `message` — an `undefined` member and a `bigint` member of the same literal both arrive as the literal — so branch on `message` to tell those apart.

+A default *value* that can't be serialized to JSON goes through the same handler, as the `.default()` schema. Under `"any"` the default is dropped and the rest of the schema is emitted as usual; return a JSON Schema to supply your own.
+
+```ts
+z.toJSONSchema(z.bigint().default(0n), {
+  unrepresentable: ({ zodSchema }) => {
+    const def = zodSchema._zod.def;
+    if (def.type === "bigint") return { type: "integer", format: "int64" };
+    if (def.type === "default") return { default: String(def.defaultValue) };
+    return "throw";
+  },
+});
+// => { type: "integer", format: "int64", default: "0" }
+```
+
 Returning a JSON Schema for a literal replaces the whole literal, dropping its representable members: `z.literal(["a", 1n])` becomes just what you returned, and `"a"` is gone. Return `"any"` instead to keep the existing per-value behavior.

 ### `cycles`
